OCRA.ttf (font H): OCR-A by John Sauter, 2004, traced from the OCR-A METAFONT sources
(Tor Lillqvist, Richard B. Wales) after ANSI X3.17-1977. Taken from Debian's
fonts-ocr-a 1.0-12 (http://sourceforge.net/projects/ocr-a-font), whose copyright file
reads:

    This software is released into the public domain.

OCRB.otf (font E): ocrb10.otf from CTAN's ocr-b-outline package (Z. Wagner, 2010),
traced from the OCR-B METAFONT sources by Norbert Schwarz, Ruhr-Universitaet Bochum.
Its README reads:

    The license of the outline fonts is the same as for the original fonts,
    i.e. you may freely use, modify, and/or distribute any of these files,
    without limitation.

The METAFONT source (CTAN fonts/ocr-b, ocrbdef.mf) carries the same terms:

    Copyright 1995, 2010 Norbert Schwarz
    You may freely use, modify and/or distribute this file, without limitation.

Changes made here (fontTools): the conversion named the glyphs at the METAFONT codes 0-31
and 128-137 after the T1 encoding, so its cmap mapped the national characters to the
wrong code points (Ä as U+010C, ß as U+0131, ...), ' to nothing and ` to a Gamma. The
glyphs were renamed after what they draw and the cmap rebuilt: ASCII, Ä Ö Ü ä ö ü ß Æ Ø
æ ø Œ œ § ¤, and U+2013 / U+2019 / U+201A / U+201D on the hyphen, apostrophe, comma and
quotation mark. " is the METAFONT's taller second quotation mark (code 137). The font was
then subset to those characters.
